---

<Post slug="wispctl-2-1-2">
	<p>
		wispctl 2.1.2 is a security fix for the eMudhra certificate authority
		compromise. the private keys of several eMudhra intermediate CAs leaked,
		so anyone holding them can issue certificates that chain up to the
		"emSign ECC Root CA - G3" root.
	</p>

	<p>
		wispctl normally trusts whatever your operating system trusts, so on most
		machines the fix is just updating your OS certificates. the exception is
		a linux machine with no CA bundle at all: debian-slim without
		<code>ca-certificates</code>, busybox, nixery images without
		<code>cacert</code>. there wispctl falls back to the roots built into the
		binary, and from 2.0.0 through 2.1.1 those still include the emSign root.
		2.1.2 drops it.
	</p>

	<h2>do i need to do anything?</h2>

	<p>
		if your CI runs in one of those slim images and pins a wispctl version,
		yes, bump it to 2.1.2. this includes preview workflows made from the
		dashboard before today, which pin <code>wispctl@2.1.1</code>.
	</p>

	<CodeBlock lang="bash" code="npm install -g wispctl@2.1.2" />

	<p>
		if you download the binary straight from sites.wisp.place, you already
		have 2.1.2 and don't need to change anything.
	</p>
</Post>
